### 1.4 Network Scanning Protocols & Nmap Command Syntax Mastery

Nmap is an open-source tool used for active host discovery, port enumeration, service version detection, and attack surface assessment. Understanding specific Nmap execution flags and underlying network interactions is essential for identifying exposure points and interpreting audit output.

#### Nmap Scanning Flags & Operational Reference

|   |   |   |   |
|---|---|---|---|
|Flag|Scan Type / Function|Mechanism & Network Description|Operational Trade-off / Notes|
|`nmap <target>`|Default SYN Scan|Scans top 1000 ports using stealthy TCP SYN packets.|Fast baseline evaluation; requires elevated privileges for raw sockets.|
|`-p <port(s)>`|Target Port Selection|Restricts execution to specified single ports or explicit port lists (e.g., `-p 22,80,443`).|Increases scanning speed by bypassing non-targeted services.|
|`-p-`|Full Port Range Scan|Forces Nmap to probe all 65,535 TCP ports.|Maximizes thoroughness; significantly increases execution duration and log noise.|
|`-sT`|TCP Connect Scan|Completes the full three-way TCP handshake (`SYN` \rightarrow `SYN-ACK` \rightarrow `ACK`).|Slower; leaves clear entries in target application access logs; used when raw sockets are unavailable.|
|`-sS`|TCP SYN Scan|Sends `SYN`, waits for `SYN-ACK`, then responds with `RST` to tear down the connection without completing the handshake.|Stealthier scan type; avoids opening full application connections on the target system.|
|`-sU`|UDP Scan|Transmits connectionless UDP probes; infers open ports from lack of response or application payloads.|Slower than TCP scanning; relies on ICMP port unreachable messages to evaluate closed states.|
|`-A`|Aggressive Scan|Combines OS detection (`-O`), service versioning (`-sV`), script scanning (`-sC`), and traceroute.|Provides comprehensive target profile data; highly noisy and easily detected by IDS/IPS systems.|
|`-sV`|Service Version Detection|Interrogates open ports with service-specific probes to extract exact banner details and software versions.|Essential for identifying vulnerable software revisions; increases scan time per host.|
|`-O`|OS Fingerprinting|Analyzes TCP/IP stack response signatures to determine target operating system details.|Useful for OS inventorying; requires at least one open and one closed port for accurate matching.|
|`-T0` to `-T5`|Timing Templates|Adjusts scan speed: `-T0` (Paranoid), `-T1` (Sneaky), `-T2` (Polite), `-T3` (Normal), `-T4` (Aggressive), `-T5` (Insane).|Lower timing levels bypass IDS detection; higher speeds expedite enterprise scanning.|
|`-sn`|Ping Scan|Performs host discovery only (ICMP echo/ARP), disabling port scanning entirely.|Rapidly maps active network hosts without probing services.|
|`-n`|Disable DNS Resolution|Skips reverse DNS lookup queries for all discovered IP addresses.|Reduces scan duration and prevents external DNS query leaks.|
|`-sL`|List Scan|Simply lists network targets without sending packets directly to them.|Operates as a non-intrusive target verification mechanism.|
|`--max-retries`|Execution Control|Limits probe retry attempts for unresponsive ports (e.g., `--max-retries 2`).|Speeds up scans across firewalled or rate-limited hosts.|
|`--host-timeout`|Timeout Modifier|Abandons slow or non-responsive targets after a set time (e.g., `--host-timeout 30m`).|Prevents single unresponsive hosts from delaying enterprise scan schedules.|

#### Real-World Nmap Execution Analysis

##### 1. Comprehensive Infrastructure Audit

```bash
nmap -A -T4 example.com
```

- **Execution Purpose:** Conducts an aggressive, high-speed audit of `example.com`.
- **Mechanism & Output:** Applies timing profile `-T4` for fast execution. The scan enables OS fingerprinting (`-O`), extracts software application version banners (`-sV`), runs default NSE scripts (`-sC`), and maps the path route (`traceroute`). Outputs open TCP ports, running service versions, underlying OS details, and potential script-detected flaws.

##### 2. Targeted Network Service Enumeration

```bash
nmap -p 22,80,443 192.168.1.0/24
```

- **Execution Purpose:** Scans a Class C subnet (`192.168.1.0/24`) specifically for common management and web service ports.
- **Mechanism & Output:** Sends probes strictly to TCP ports 22 (SSH), 80 (HTTP), and 443 (HTTPS) across all 254 host IP addresses. Generates a concise host-by-host summary detailing the open/closed/filtered status of these three target management ports.

##### 3. Stealthy Application Version Probe

```bash
nmap -sS -sV example.com
```

- **Execution Purpose:** Discretely identifies running software versions on `example.com`.
- **Mechanism & Output:** Executes a half-open TCP SYN stealth scan (`-sS`) to identify open ports without completing full three-way handshakes. Interrogates open ports with version probes (`-sV`) to determine the exact application names and version numbers running on the server.

